Z||000000||enslaved||09-01-2001||04:32 PM||||I have a problem on my hands here. I am replacing all my brakes. The front ones went fine. I have a problem with the rear ones though. I took off the old ones with no problems, but when i went to put the new inner pad on, i ran into something not cool. I cant get that damn retainer clip to stay on for the life of me!!!! and its soooooo damn frusterating!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! Does anybody have any ideas? I am about to throw a hammer at my car.||||reg||1
Z||000001||enslaved||09-01-2001||05:31 PM||||And i forgot to mention, i cant get the piston to go back into the bore! do i absolutly have to remove the e-brake lever? please help me with some answers! ||||reg||9
Z||000002||Kasdan||09-01-2001||06:14 PM||||There is a piston tool.... for the rear....<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FIERO2.JPG">       <BR>Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Conrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000003||enslaved||09-01-2001||07:15 PM||||wow, heh, thanks for the in depth reply  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I've heard you can use a C-clamp to do it just fine. But it seems like theres something blocking the piston from going any further into the bore. How do i make it go further?||||reg||1
