Z||000005||enslaved||09-01-2001||10:50 PM||||OK, I took the lever off, and the washers, and used a C-clamp to push the piston back into the bore. Its back there. Now, The little pad retainer doesn't fit right! I guess i have to buy a new one. I am still affraid that i hurt my caliper during the e-brake lever removal process. I had to take the caliper off the rotor to get enough leverage on the damn nut for the lever. I cant believe thats the way the brake shops and pro's have to change the pads on those calipers.. it just seems wayyyyyy too time consuming. It feels like i'm never going to get my car back on the road! GRRRRRRRRR!||||reg||1
Z||000006||David DeVoe||09-02-2001||08:57 AM||||enslaved<BR>Im pretty sure that there is a tool that turns the piston and that the turning action moves it back into the caliper. My manual says that rear pistons must be turned to be retracted.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||09-02-2001||09:25 AM||||Your right!  There is a tool.  It has two notches in it that fits on the face of the piston.  The tool hooks up to a socket wrench and by turning and pushing the piston it will totate back into the caliper.<P>Its designed like this because of the parking brake mechanism.  The parking brake is mechanical, and doesn't use fluid pressure to hold on the brakes.  To do this there is a screw-drive mechanism in the piston.  You have to re-set it by screwing it back in when you bottom the piston.<P>If you hurt the caliper, you will know because it either won't work, or it will leak fluid.  Rebuilds aren't too expensive, or you can rebuild it yourself.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-02-2001||08:18 PM||||If those pistons have rubber "plugs" in the face then DO NOT rotate them back. Used the clamp method. I'm still trying to find reliable data for the plugless pistons. I think they are save to turn but I can't say for sure.<P>The clips can be a pain. They sometimes need to be bent a bit in or out. If you look at the 4 ears that hold the pad, 2 have a slight bend on the end. These go on last.<P>If your pistons don't have the 4 notches for the sheet metal clip then Carefully pry it over the piston. Put three ears into the groove and pry over the last one.||||reg||1
Z||000009||enslaved||09-02-2001||09:09 PM||||Well, ogre, i've read a lot of the brake posts, and i trust your judgement when it comes to brake work. I got the piston back in the bore.. It did turn a little bit to the left when i used the c-clamp, but i stuck needle nosed plyers into the d-slots and turned it back to its original vertical position. I ordered pad-to-piston clip today, getting it tommorow. The old one had to clips on the top, and the little bent out part for the notch on the bottom of the pad. I am replacing it because the little part sticking out wouldn't straddle the notch on the pad, and i was affraid it would come off, and beat my rotor to hell when im driving. I just prey I didn't wreck my caliper!!!!!! One thing i noticed, is when i turn the e-brake lever now, up or down, the piston moves out regardless of the way i push turn it. Is this a very bad thing?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-02-2001||11:53 PM||||Is it posible you damaged a piston? yes. Did you? Don't Know.<P>Don't fool with the hand brake screw any more than needed with the system apart. There is a big spring in there that pushes the pistons out. It's probably the cause of what your describing. when I'm going to do work that requires rear calier compression... My rule is to remove the hand brake arms first and replace them last. <P>btw... You don't want those actuator nuts to be loose. ever. they are lock nuts for a reason. If they back off the caliper can fail. The actuator screw will get dragged into the caliper untill the seal eventually quits. Very nasty mess....<P>The best thing I can tell you would be...<P>put the system together. Do the manual rear brake adjustment. then see how everything works. (The shime described in that article IS REQUIRED! Do Not attempt it w/o the shim!)<P>See how the pedal feels. If the pedal is way off as soon as you start it then find out why. The normal height of the Loaded brake pedal is to be above the gas pedal's resting height.<P>If you think there is a problem. Block the wheels in both directions. Not tight, leave enough room to roll a few inches. (Use big block that are hard to roll over. Chunks of 4x4 or big rocks.) Start it and don't touch the gas pedal. Push the brakes and put it in gear. Make sure it can hold the car, and again see how the pedal feels.<P>Drive it slowly for a 1/2 mile or so and stop several times doing it. If there are any major issues or caliper damage it should show up right away.<P>pull it back in the driveway and jack the rear wheels. There should be little or no drag when you spin the wheels. If they drag then you need to figure out why. (Both wheels have to be jacked or the car has to be out of gear/park.)<P>the rear calipers are dificult to explain. They are designed to work the pistons over very short distances. They have definat issues when the pads are changed or if you work the hand brake while the system is appart. <P>I've found that both versions of rear pistons require the manual adjustment procedure after caliper/pad replacement. They simply want to be trolls and not do it for you. This single item makes a huge difference in brake performance. I now do it every time I have the calipers off, even just for clean and grease. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||enslaved||09-03-2001||01:17 AM||||Ok, thanks man, i'll have to give it a shot. I have a very bad feeling about all this though. This is WAAAAYYY more trouble than it should be. I just dont believe that the damn brake shops take their time taking the caliper apart just to change the pads. just plain doesn't make sense. but thanks again.||||reg||1
